# Kernel config fragment merged onto the riscv defconfig (cmake/boot-image.cmake).
#
# The console lives on the design's AXI UART Lite. The command line comes from the
# device tree's /chosen node, which the SoC generator emits (console selection and
# earlycon describe the design's UART) - no CONFIG_CMDLINE is baked in, so one kernel
# image serves any hardware generation. HZ is pinned so the timeconst generation is
# deterministic across hosts. The rootfs is an initramfs embedded into the Image -
# the SD controller has no mainline driver, so there is no storage for Linux to mount.
# The initramfs description is a FIXED path in the kernel build dir; each boot image
# copies its own description there, so switching programs never reconfigures.
# AppArmor is useless on this SoC and its capability_names.h generation needs GNU
# sed (\L), which BSD/macOS sed lacks - the only known GNU-sed site in the build.
# CONFIG_SECURITY_APPARMOR is not set
CONFIG_SERIAL_UARTLITE=y
CONFIG_SERIAL_UARTLITE_CONSOLE=y
# All fabric peripheral interrupts cascade through an AXI INTC into the PLIC (the PLIC's
# level-only gateways lose the UART Lite's edge pulses); the INTC is a chained irqchip,
# so without this driver NO peripheral interrupt - console included - ever fires.
CONFIG_XILINX_INTC=y
# The VDMA of video-stream designs (dmaengine driver; probes via the device tree).
CONFIG_XILINX_DMA=y
# The USB host controller in the processing system. Its Synopsys core binds the plain
# `snps,dwc3` node the generator emits, with no vendor glue: the Xilinx wrapper driver
# configures clocks, resets and the PHY through the ZynqMP firmware interface, an APU-side
# service this SoC does not run, and psu_init has done all of it already. Host only - the
# board's ULPI transceiver supports no other role, and the device tree pins dr_mode to match.
# xHCI, mass storage, SCSI and evdev already come from the riscv defconfig.
CONFIG_USB_DWC3=y
CONFIG_USB_DWC3_HOST=y
# The USB controller's DMA reaches only DRAM's first 512 MB; the device tree reserves a
# `restricted-dma-pool` inside that window and this makes the kernel bounce the controller's
# transfers through it. Without it, buffers beyond the window bounce through the DEFAULT
# swiotlb pool, which on a large-memory board itself sits beyond the window - every USB
# transfer then fails ("swiotlb buffer is full ... used 0 slots").
CONFIG_DMA_RESTRICTED_POOL=y
# Keyboards and mice. The defconfig enables these today; they are named here because input is
# the point of having USB at all, and a default is not a guarantee.
CONFIG_HID=y
CONFIG_USB_HID=y
CONFIG_HID_GENERIC=y
CONFIG_HZ_250=y
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_INITRD=y
CONFIG_INITRAMFS_SOURCE="@KBUILD_DESC@"
